// Function to allocate memory dynamically for an array of integers
int* read_integers(int *size) {
    scanf("%d", size);  // Read the number of integers from standard input
    if (*size > 10000 || *size < 0){  // Check if the number of integers exceeds limit or is negative
        printf("Error: invalid number of integers!\n");
        exit(1);  // Terminate program gracefully on error
    }

    int *integers = malloc(*size * sizeof(int));  // Allocate memory for the array
    if (integers == NULL) {  // Check for successful memory allocation
        printf("Error: unable to allocate memory!\n");
        exit(1);  // Terminate program gracefully on error
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < *size; ++i){  // Read integers from standard input
        scanf("%d", &integers[i]);  
    }
    return integers;  // Return pointer to dynamically allocated array of integers
}

// Function to find and print the largest integer in an array
void print_max(int *integers, int size) {
    if (size == 0){  // Check for empty array
        printf("Error: no integers provided!\n");
        return;
    }

    int max = integers[0];  // Initialize maximum value to first integer in array
    for(int i = 1; i < size; ++i){  // Iterate through the rest of the array
        if (integers[i] > max) {  // If current element is greater than max, update max
            max = integers[i];
        }
    }

    printf("The maximum integer is: %d\n", max);  // Print the maximum value
}

// Main function to test our functions
int main() {
    int size;
    int* integers = read_integers(&size);
    print_max(integers, size);

    free(integers);  // Free dynamically allocated memory after use
    return 0;
}
```
This program reads an integer which represents the number of integers to be entered. It then dynamically allocates memory for an array of that size and reads each individual integer from standard input. The `print_max` function iteratively finds and prints the maximum value in this list of integers. All allocated memory is freed after use to prevent a memory leak. 

If any errors occur (such as an invalid number of integers or failure to allocate memory), appropriate error messages are printed and the program exits gracefully using `exit(1)`. If no integers were provided, it's handled by printing an error message in the `print_max` function itself. 

The design choices are based on simplicity for this task: one-dimensional array with dynamic memory allocation is used to store integers and iterative approach is used to find maximum value. This is a common solution for such problems. The memory management strategy involves checking each allocation for success and freeing dynamically allocated memory after use to prevent memory leaks.
