Please note that real-time transaction processing is a complex task and should be handled with care to avoid data loss or performance bottlenecks. Further optimizations can be done by using lock-free data structures for multi-threading, reducing memory allocation/deallocation, etc. 

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <pthread.h>

// Define a transaction structure
typedef struct {
    time_t timestamp;
    int id;
    float amount;
    char category[20];
} Transaction;

// Define a metric structure
typedef struct {
    unsigned long totalCount;
    double totalAmount;
    float maxWeightedSum;
} Metrics;

// Define the filter parameters
int MAX_VOLUME = 50;
double AGE_THRESHOLD = 5.0; // in seconds
float weights[20] = { /* Some predefined weights */ };

pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

Metrics metrics;

void processTransaction(Transaction transaction) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    time_t now = time(NULL);
    double age = difftime(now, transaction.timestamp);

    // Filter transactions based on age and volume
    if (age <= AGE_THRESHOLD) { 
        metrics.totalCount++;
        metrics.totalAmount += transaction.amount;

        float weightedSum = weights[transaction.category] * transaction.amount;

        // Check for numerical stability and update maxWeightedSum
        if (metrics.maxWeightedSum < weightedSum) { 
            metrics.maxWeightedSum = weightedSum;
        }
    } else {
        printf("Transaction is too old\n");
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
To implement the real-time filtering system and logging, a more complex solution would be needed. You'd likely need to use some form of queuing or buffer for incoming transactions and processing them asynchronously in separate threads. 

