['To implement this in Rust, we can use `std::env` to manage environment variables and `tempfile` to create temporary directories. Here is an example of how you might approach this:

```rust
use std::env;
use std::fs::remove_dir_all;
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};
use std::path::PathBuf;
use tempfile::TempDir;
use std::thread;

#[derive(Debug)]
struct TestEnv {
    dir: Arc<Mutex<Option<PathBuf>>>,
}

impl TestEnv {
    fn new() -> Self {
        let test_id = env::var("TEST_ID").unwrap();
        let temp_dir = TempDir::new().expect("Could not create temporary directory");

        let path = temp_dir.into_path();
        env::set_var("TEST_ID", &test_id);
        Self { dir: Arc::new(Mutex::new(Some(path))) }
    }

    fn get(&self) -> Option<PathBuf> {
        let mut path = self.dir.lock().unwrap();
        match *path {
            None => None,
            Some(ref p) => Some(p.clone()),
        }
    }
}

impl Drop for TestEnv {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        if let Some(path) = self.get() {
            thread::spawn(move || {
                remove_dir_all(&path).expect("Could not clean up temporary directory");
            });
        }
    }
}
```
In this code, we define a struct `TestEnv` that holds the path to the test's unique ID. This struct also implements `Drop` which is called when the struct goes out of scope, in this case at the end of each test run. In the drop implementation, we spawn a new thread to remove the temporary directory asynchronously.

Please note: Rust has strict rules around async code that might not be followed by some environments (like `tokio` runtime), so it may need adjustments based on where your code will run. Also, this is a simplified example and doesn't include error handling or concurrency control. You should add those parts according to your needs.
