['Here is a basic Java code snippet for this task. Please note that this solution doesn't include the implementation of MoSTState class or actual checksum computation due to it being not specified in the question.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.*;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import org.apache.commons.codec.digest.DigestUtils;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

public class FileHandler {
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(FileHandler.class);
    private static final MessageDigest DIGEST = DigestUtils.getSha256Digest();

    public synchronized Integer[] handleFile(Path filepath, Path checksumPath) {
        List<Integer> numbers = new ArrayList<>();
        try (BufferedReader reader = Files.newBufferedReader(filepath)) {
            String line;
            while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                if (!isValidNumber(line)) continue;
                numbers.add(Integer.parseInt(line));
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            logger.error("Failed to read file", e);
            return null;
        }

        String checksum = computeChecksum(filepath);
        if (!checksumsMatch(checksum, checksumPath)) {
            logger.warn("File has been modified");
            return null;
        }

        Collections.sort(numbers);
        Integer[] sortedNumbers = numbers.toArray(new Integer[0]);
        Arrays.sort(sortedNumbers);

        return sortedNumbers;
    }

    private boolean isValidNumber(String line) {
        // Implement your own logic here, you could check if the string can be parsed into an integer
        try {
            Integer.parseInt(line);
            return true;
        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            return false;
        }
    }

    private String computeChecksum(Path filepath) {
        try (InputStream in = Files.newInputStream(filepath)) {
            byte[] buffer = new byte[8192];
            int len = 0;
            while ((len = in.read(buffer)) != -1) {
                DIGEST.update(buffer, 0, len);
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            logger.error("Failed to compute checksum", e);
        }

        return new String(DIGEST.digest());
    }

    private boolean checksumsMatch(String actualChecksum, Path expectedChecksumPath) {
        try (BufferedReader reader = Files.newBufferedReader(expectedChecksumPath)) {
            String expectedChecksum = reader.readLine();
            return actualChecksum.equals(expectedChecksum);
        } catch (IOException e) {
            logger.error("Failed to read checksum file", e);
            return false;
        }
    }
}
```
This code utilizes Java's `BufferedReader` for efficient reading, and `Collections.sort()` for sorting the list of numbers. The synchronization keyword ensures that this method can be accessed concurrently by multiple threads without causing race conditions or inconsistent data. This implementation also supports large files as it reads and processes them line by line, reducing memory usage. 

In order to handle potential corruptions, a checksum is computed for the file content and compared with the stored checksum in a separate metadata file. If they do not match, the method logs a warning message and returns null. Please note that this code uses Apache Commons library to compute SHA-256 checksums of files. You'll need to add it as a dependency in your project.
