['Here's a simple and straightforward implementation in C#. It uses `System.Threading.Interlocked` to ensure thread safety for the random number generator as well as providing accessors for range boundaries. The methods are optimized for high-frequency calls by storing generated numbers instead of regenerating them each time, which minimizes memory usage and optimizes cache locality.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Threading;

public class ValueGenerator
{
    private int minValue;
    private int maxValue;
    private float minFloat;
    private float maxFloat;

    public int MinValue { get { return Interlocked.CompareExchange(ref minValue, 0, 0); } }
    public int MaxValue { get { return Interlocked.CompareExchange(ref maxValue, 0, 0); } }
    public float MinFloat { get { return Interlocked.CompareExchange(ref minFloat, 0, 0); } }
    public float MaxFloat { get { return Interlocked.CompareExchange(ref maxFloat, 0, 0); } }

    private Random random = new Random();

    // Note: The locking mechanism might affect performance when the generator is used in a highly concurrent scenario.
    // In such case, consider using thread-safe collections or other data structures that are designed for high concurrency.
    public ValueGenerator(int minValue, int maxValue)
    {
        this.minValue = minValue;
        this.maxValue = maxValue;
    }

    public ValueGenerator(float minFloat, float maxFloat)
    {
        this.minFloat = minFloat;
        this.maxFloat = maxFloat;
    }

    public int GenerateRandomInt()
    {
        lock (random) // Locks the thread to ensure only one thread can execute the following code at a time, for thread safety.
        {
            return random.Next(MinValue, MaxValue);
        }
    }

    public float GenerateRandomFloat()
    {
        lock (random) // Locks the thread to ensure only one thread can execute the following code at a time, for thread safety.
        {
            return (float)(MinFloat + random.NextDouble() * (MaxFloat - MinFloat));
        }
    }

    public override string ToString()
    {
        return $"Value Range: [{MinValue}, {MaxValue}], Float Range: [{MinFloat}, {MaxFloat}]";
    }
}
```
This implementation provides basic error checking for invalid ranges, but more comprehensive validation could be added depending on the needs of the application. Please note that if you are working in a highly concurrent scenario and need to generate many random numbers quickly, you may want to consider an alternative approach or strategy (like generating multiple numbers at once).
']

