['Below is a simplified implementation of the given requirements. It uses Java's NIO File API for efficient file handling, along with Apache Commons IO library to handle symbolic links and permissions issues. The code also uses a ConcurrentHashMap to ensure thread safety while updating the map. However, due to the complexity of this task, it is recommended to seek further assistance from experienced developers or engage in discussions on forums dedicated to Java programming to get a more comprehensive solution.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.nio.file.attribute.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import org.apache.commons.io.FileUtils;

public class DirectoryProcessor {
    private static ConcurrentHashMap<String, Long> fileSizeMap = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public static void processDirectory(Path dirPath, int maxDepth, String followLinks) throws IOException {
        Files.walkFileTree(dirPath, EnumSet.noneOf(FileVisitOption.class), maxDepth, 
            new SimpleFileVisitor<Path>() {
                @Override
                public FileVisitResult visitFileFailed(Path file, IOException exc) throws IOException {
                    System.err.println("Unable to access: " + file);
                    return FileVisitResult.CONTINUE;
                }

                @Override
                public FileVisitResult preVisitDirectory(Path dir, BasicFileAttributes attrs) throws IOException {
                    if (!Files.isReadable(dir)) {
                        System.err.println("Unreadable directory: " + dir);
                        return FileVisitResult.SKIP_SUBTREE;
                    } else {
                        return FileVisitResult.CONTINUE;
                    }
                }

                @Override
                public FileVisitResult visitFile(Path file, BasicFileAttributes attrs) throws IOException {
                    if (Files.isRegularFile(file) && Files.isReadable(file)) {
                        long size = Files.size(file);

                        if (followLinks.equals("ignore") && Files.isSymbolicLink(file)) {
                            return FileVisitResult.CONTINUE;
                        } else if (followLinks.equals("report")) {
                            fileSizeMap.putIfAbsent(FileUtils.readSymlink(file).toString(), size);
                        } else if (Files.isSymbolicLink(file)) {
                            Path target = FileUtils.readSymbolicLink(Paths.get(dir.toUri()).relativize(Paths.get(FileUtils.readSymlink(file).toString())).toFile().getAbsolutePath());

                            if (Files.exists(target)) {
                                fileSizeMap.putIfAbsent(target.toString(), size);
                            } else {
                                System.err.println("Broken symlink: " + file);
                            }
                        } else {
                            fileSizeMap.putIfAbsent(file.toString(), size);
                        }
                    }

                    return FileVisitResult.CONTINUE;
                }
            });
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        Path dirPath = Paths.get("directory path here");
        int maxDepth = 30; // Set your preferred max depth
        String followLinks = "follow"; // follow, ignore or report

        processDirectory(dirPath, maxDepth, followLinks);

        System.out.println(fileSizeMap);
    }
}
```
This code snippet will scan a directory up to the specified depth, and it can handle symbolic links according to user preference (follow them, ignore them or report as separate entries). The size of each file is put into ConcurrentHashMap where key is the file path and value is the file size. Please replace "directory path here" with your directory path string.
']

['Here's a basic outline of how you might approach this task using Java's `Path`, `Files`, `AtomicInteger`, `CompletableFuture` for concurrency control, and `MessageDigest` for checksum generation. 

```Java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;
import java.util.stream.Stream;
import java.security.NoSuchAlgorithmException;

public class FileProcessor {
    private final AtomicInteger checksum = new AtomicInteger(0);

    public String processFile(Path file, Path metaData) throws IOException {
        // Check for corrupted or missing files
        if (!Files.exists(file)) {
            System.out.println("File not found");
            return null;
        }

        // Validate checksum 
        try (InputStream in = Files.newInputStream(metaData)) {
            byte[] buffer = new byte[1024];
            MessageDigest complete = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");

            int numRead;
            do {
                numRead = in.read(buffer);

                if (numRead > 0) {
                    complete.update(buffer, 0, numRead);
                }
            } while (numRead != -1);

            byte[] digest = complete.digest();
            String md5sum = new BigInteger(1, digest).toString(16);

            if (!md5sum.equals(checksum)) {
                System.out.println("Warning: Checksums do not match");
                return null;
            }
        } catch (IOException | NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }

        // Parallel processing 
        try(Stream<String> stream = Files.lines(file)) {
            AtomicInteger count = new AtomicInteger(0);

            CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> {
                stream.forEach(line -> {
                    System.out.println("Line " + count.incrementAndGet() + ": " + line);
                    // Sort and extract data here
                });

                return file;
            }).exceptionally((throwable) -> {
                throwable.printStackTrace();
                System.out.println("Error processing file");
                return null;
            });
        }

        // Sequential Processing 
        try(Stream<String> stream = Files.lines(file)) {
            AtomicInteger count = new AtomicInteger(0);

            stream.forEach(line -> {
                System.out.println("Line " + count.incrementAndGet() + ": " + line);
                // Sort and extract data here
            });
        }

        return file;
    }
}
```
Please note that this is a basic implementation of the task and does not take into account all potential cases or optimizations. For example, it doesn't handle temporary I/O failures (you might want to use an automated retry mechanism for that), nor does it validate whether a file is indeed in the expected format before processing.
']

