In Rust we would use the `tokio`, `clap` and `regex` crates for asynchronous execution, command-line arguments, and filename handling respectively:

Please note that this is a simplified version of what you might have in mind, and it may need adjustment to fit your exact requirements. This version assumes that filenames with no extension are valid but filenames with invalid characters are not. Also, the unique identifier length will be half the original filename's length for simplicity.

```rust
use std::fs;
use tokio::task::spawn_blocking;
use clap::Parser;
use regex::Regex;
use md5::compute_md5;
use anyhow::Result;

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<()> {
    let args = Args::parse();

    spawn_blocking(move || {
        rename_file(&args.filename)?;
        Ok(())
    })
    .await??;

    Ok(())
