['Here is an example of how you can implement this in Java using the SLF4J library for logging, Apache Commons IO for handling files and directories, Guava EventBus for event-driven programming to handle log events, and Retryer from Jcabi for implementing a retry mechanism:

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.*;
import com.google.common.eventbus.*;
import com.jcabi.aspects.*;
import org.apache.commons.io.*;
import org.slf4j.*;

public class DirectoryProcessor {
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(DirectoryProcessor.class);
    private EventBus eventBus;

    public DirectoryProcessor() {
        this.eventBus = new EventBus();
        this.eventBus.register(new ConsoleLogEventHandler());
    }

    public Map<String, Long> processDirectories(List<String> filePaths) throws IOException {
        Map<String, Long> directorySummaryMap = new HashMap<>();

        for (String filePath : filePaths) {
            try {
                File file = new File(filePath);

                if (!file.exists()) {
                    logger.warn("File does not exist: " + filePath);
                    continue;
                }

                long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
                String directoryPath = FilenameUtils.getFullPath(filePath);

                Retryer<Object> retryer = new Retryer<>() {
                    @Override
                    public Object tryCommunicate() throws Exception {
                        long fileSize = FileUtils.sizeOf(file);

                        if (directorySummaryMap.containsKey(directoryPath)) {
                            directorySummaryMap.put(directoryPath, directorySummaryMap.get(directoryPath) + fileSize);
                        } else {
                            directorySummaryMap.put(directoryPath, fileSize);
                        }

                        long endTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
                        eventBus.post(new LogEvent("Processed " + directoryPath + " in " + (endTime - startTime) + " ms"));

                        return null;
                    }
                };

                retryer.retry(5, 1000);
            } catch (Exception e) {
                logger.error("Failed to process file: " + filePath, e);
            }
        }

        return directorySummaryMap;
    }

    private static class ConsoleLogEventHandler {
        @Subscribe
        public void handle(LogEvent event) {
            System.out.println("Console - " + event.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```
This implementation assumes you're using Maven or Gradle to manage your dependencies, and that you have appropriate logging configurations set up in your project. It also assumes a retry limit of 5 attempts with 1 second delay between each attempt for the file processing. You might need to adjust these values based on your specific requirements.
']

["Below is an example of how this might be implemented using Apache's HttpClient library. Please note that you would have to install this in your project. The code does not implement the complete logic as it requires a lot more context, but I hope this gives a good start and understanding about how such system should look like from a high level perspective:

```java
import org.apache.http.*;
import org.apache.http.client.config.RequestConfig;
import org.apache.http.conn.ConnectionPoolTimeoutException;
import org.apache.http.impl.client.CloseableHttpClient;
import org.apache.http.impl.client.HttpClients;
import org.apache.http.impl.conn.PoolingHttpClientConnectionManager;

public class ConnectionPool {
    private CloseableHttpClient httpClient;
    private PoolingHttpClientConnectionManager connManager;

    public ConnectionPool() {
        RequestConfig requestConfig = RequestConfig.custom()
                .setConnectTimeout(5000)
                .setSocketTimeout(5000)
                .build();

        connManager = new PoolingHttpClientConnectionManager();
        connManager.setDefaultMaxPerRoute(10);
        connManager.setMaxTotal(20);

        httpClient = HttpClients.custom()
                .setDefaultRequestConfig(requestConfig)
                .setConnectionManager(connManager)
                .build();
    }

    public void executeRequest(HttpUriRequest request, ResponseHandler<? extends Object> responseHandler) {
        try {
            httpClient.execute(request, responseHandler);
        } catch (IOException e) {
            // Handle exception
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    public void shutdown() throws IOException {
        httpClient.close();
        connManager.shutdown();
    }
}
```
This is just an example to show how you might start managing connections with a connection pool, but it's not fully functional as-is and will need to be extended significantly to meet all the requirements. Also note that this does not cover load balancing or priority based allocation of connections which would require more in-depth knowledge about your specific use case and infrastructure.
