["Here is the C code for your task. This code uses a mutex for thread-safety and avoids potential race conditions due to possible memory corruption when multiple threads are working on it at the same time. 

Please note that you will need to implement the Session API and Session data structure yourself, as these are left out of this basic example. The code below only provides a skeleton function for creating sessions, not actual session management functions.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct Session {
    int id;
    // Add other fields as needed by your application
    struct Session *next;
} Session;

// Assuming SessionManagementAPI is an API for managing sessions, replace it with the actual structure of your api 
typedef void* SessionManagementAPI; 

pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
Session *sessions = NULL; // The list of all existing sessions

// This function creates a new session and returns it, or NULL if there is insufficient memory.
Session* createSession(int id) {
    Session *newSession = (Session*)malloc(sizeof(Session)); 

    if (newSession == NULL) {
        return NULL; // Insufficient memory
    }

    newSession->id = id;
    newSession->next = NULL;

    return newSession;
}

// This function creates a list of sessions from an array of session IDs. It also updates the 'next' pointer 
// of the last existing session to point at the newly created session list.
Session* manageSessions(int *sessionIds, int numOfSessions, SessionManagementAPI api, int lastId) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex); // Ensure thread-safety while modifying shared state

    Session **tail = &sessions;
    for (int i = 0; i < numOfSessions; i++) {
        *tail = createSession(sessionIds[i]);

        if (*tail == NULL) {
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex); // Release the lock on shared state, as something went wrong.
            return NULL; // Insufficient memory
        }

        tail = &((*tail)->next); // Move to next pointer in list
    }

    Session *lastSession = createSession(lastId);

    if (lastSession == NULL) {
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex); // Release the lock on shared state, as something went wrong.
        return NULL; // Insufficient memory
    }

    // Update 'next' pointer of last existing session to point at newly created sessions.
    *tail = lastSession; 

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex); // Release the lock on shared state, as we are done modifying it.

    return sessions; // Return the head of the new list
}
```
This code is a basic starting point and may need adjustments to fit your specific needs. It assumes that SessionManagementAPI is some API for managing sessions which you will have to replace with an actual definition. The function `createSession` creates a new session structure, and `manageSessions` uses it to create a linked list of session structures from an array of IDs. These functions handle thread-safety using a mutex to avoid race conditions due to possible memory corruption when multiple threads are working on shared state concurrently.
