['This task requires knowledge of C programming, external libraries, and system calls. Here is an example solution using libjpeg to handle JPEG data, imagemagick to convert JPEG to PDF, and gnupg to manage password-protected file overwrites. This is a simplified version and might not cover all error conditions, but it can serve as a starting point:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <jpeglib.h>

int jpeg_to_pdf(char* input_file, char* output_file, int compression_level) {
    // Load JPEG file
    FILE *input = fopen(input_file, "rb");
    if (input == NULL) return 1;

    struct jpeg_decompress_struct cinfo;
    struct jpeg_error_mgr jerr;
    cinfo.err = jpeg_std_error(&jerr);
    jpeg_create_decompress(&cinfo);
    jpeg_stdio_src(&cinfo, input);

    // Read JPEG header and start decompression
    jpeg_read_header(&cinfo, TRUE);
    jpeg_start_decompress(&cinfo);

    // Convert to PDF using imagemagick
    char command[256];
    sprintf(command, "convert -quality %d %s %s", compression_level, input_file, output_file);
    system(command);

    jpeg_finish_decompress(&cinfo);
    jpeg_destroy_decompress(&cinfo);
    fclose(input);

    // Check if the user wants to overwrite the existing PDF file
    gpg_context_t ctx;
    char *password = getpass("Enter your password: ");

    if (gpg_checkpassphrase(ctx, password)) {
        // If the user enters the correct password, overwrite the output file
        return 0;
    } else {
        // If not, don't overwrite and clean up temporary files
        remove(output_file);
        return 1;
    }
}
```
Please note that this code has several dependencies: libjpeg for JPEG handling, imagemagick for image conversion, and gnupg for password protection. You might need to install these libraries via package managers such as apt or yum on Unix-based systems. Also, remember to handle errors properly in real-world applications. This example is kept simple for clarity.
']

