['Here is a simple implementation in C that meets these requirements. 

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <math.h>

double mean(const double* arr, size_t len) {
    double sum = 0.0;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < len; ++i) {
        sum += arr[i];
    }
    return sum / len;
}

double stddev(const double* arr, size_t len, double mean) {
    double variance = 0.0;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < len; ++i) {
        variance += pow(arr[i] - mean, 2);
    }
    return sqrt(variance / len);
}

int main() {
    size_t capacity = 100; // initial capacity of the array
    double* arr = malloc(capacity * sizeof(*arr));

    if (arr == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Memory allocation failed\n");
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    size_t len = 0; // number of elements in the array

    while (1) {
        double num;
        int ret = scanf("%lf,", &num);

        if (ret == EOF || len >= 10000) {
            break;
        }
        else if (ret == 1 && len < capacity) {
            arr[len++] = num;
        } 
        else { // need to allocate more memory
            size_t newCapacity = capacity * 2;
            double* tmp = realloc(arr, newCapacity * sizeof(*arr));

            if (tmp == NULL) {
                fprintf(stderr, "Memory allocation failed\n");
                free(arr);
                return EXIT_FAILURE;
            } 
            else {
                arr = tmp;
                capacity = newCapacity;
                arr[len++] = num;
            }
        }
    }

    if (len >= 10000) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Error: Maximum limit of numbers exceeded\n");
        free(arr);
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    double m = mean(arr, len);
    printf("Mean: %lf\n", m);

    double sd = stddev(arr, len, m);
    printf("Standard Deviation: %lf\n", sd);

    free(arr);
    return 0;
}
```
This program uses `double` to store the numbers for precision. Since we are dealing with floating-point arithmetic here, using `float` could potentially lead to loss of precision and rounding errors.

The memory management strategy is straightforward: initially, we allocate an array of size 100. Whenever this array is full and we need to add more numbers, we double the capacity by reallocating a new larger array. This ensures that we do not run out of space while still having enough headroom.

The program reads numbers in a loop until it reaches EOF (when inputting from stdin), or when 10,000 numbers have been read, whichever comes first. It checks if there is enough room for the next number before adding it to the array. If not, it doubles the capacity of the array using `realloc`.

If an error occurs (like memory allocation failing or exceeding the maximum limit), appropriate error messages are printed and the program terminates gracefully with a non-zero exit status by calling `free(arr)` before returning from main. 

Regarding performance for large datasets, this implementation reads numbers one at a time and adds them to the array as they come in, which ensures that only a constant amount of memory is used regardless of the size of the input data. This might not be efficient for very large inputs where you would need to read all the data into memory first before processing it, but it should work fine for typical use cases with input sizes up to about 10,000 numbers. If performance becomes an issue, a more advanced approach could involve reading and parsing the input in chunks rather than one number at a time, which would reduce the overhead of frequent memory allocations and reallocations.
']

