["Here is a simple implementation in C using POSIX threads (pthreads). Note that this is a simplified version and might not be suitable for all use cases due to its lack of error checking:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <unistd.h> // For usleep() (for demonstration only)

typedef struct LTE_PHY_RX_BUFFER {
    int PHY_RX_BUFFER_TAG;
    int RX_STREAM_ID;
} LTE_PHY_RX_BUFFER;

#define MAX_BUFFER_SIZE 1024 // Define your maximum size here.
LTE_PHY_RX_BUFFER buffer[MAX_BUFFER_SIZE];
int bufferCount = 0;
pthread_mutex_t bufferMutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void handle_data(int tag, int streamID) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&bufferMutex);

    if (bufferCount >= MAX_BUFFER_SIZE) {
        // Buffer is full, discard the data.
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&bufferMutex);
        return;
    }

    for(int i = 0; i < bufferCount; ++i){
        if(buffer[i].RX_STREAM_ID == streamID){
            // Duplicate RX_STREAM_ID, discard the data and release lock.
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&bufferMutex);
            return;
        }
    }

    buffer[bufferCount].PHY_RX_BUFFER_TAG = tag;
    buffer[bufferCount].RX_STREAM_ID = streamID;
    ++bufferCount;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&bufferMutex);
}
```
This implementation uses a mutex to ensure thread-safety when modifying the list. The function `handle_data` is called every time new data arrives from the RF interface, and it locks the mutex before checking if adding the new entry would exceed the maximum size or cause duplicate ID's. If there is room and no duplication, it adds the data to the buffer and increments the count. Otherwise, it simply discards the data and unlocks the mutex.
"]

