["The task description is quite extensive. Here's a basic implementation of a Device Context structure and dynamic allocation with some rudimentary concurrency control using mutexes for thread safety. For brevity, error checking has been omitted from this example. 

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <sys/types.h>

typedef struct {
    int throughput;
    float packet_loss;
} QoSMetrics;

typedef struct DeviceContext {
    char DevID[10];
    size_t serviceCount;
    char **services;
    QoSMetrics metrics;
    struct DeviceContext *next;
} DeviceContext;

DeviceContext *head = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void addService(DeviceContext *device, const char *service) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    ++device->serviceCount;
    device->services = realloc(device->services, device->serviceCount * sizeof(*device->services));
    device->services[device->serviceCount - 1] = strdup(service);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

void removeService(DeviceContext *device, const char *service) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    for (size_t i = 0; i < device->serviceCount; ++i) {
        if (!strcmp(device->services[i], service)) {
            free(device->services[i]);
            memmove(&device->services[i], &device->services[i + 1], (device->serviceCount - i - 1) * sizeof(*device->services));
            --device->serviceCount;
            break;
        }
    }
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

DeviceContext* createDevice(const char *DevID, const QoSMetrics metrics) {
    DeviceContext *new = calloc(1, sizeof(*new));
    strncpy(new->DevID, DevID, sizeof(new->DevID) - 1);
    new->metrics = metrics;
    return new;
}

void destroyDevice(DeviceContext *device) {
    for (size_t i = 0; i < device->serviceCount; ++i) {
        free(device->services[i]);
    }
    free(device->services);
    free(device);
}
```
The `addService` and `removeService` functions handle the addition and removal of service instances while maintaining thread safety. The mutex is acquired before each operation that modifies the device context to ensure consistency across threads. 

You'll need a way to adjust QoS metrics based on network congestion levels, manage dynamic changes in the network infrastructure (add/remove service instances), cleanup allocated resources and logging system which are outside the scope of this question as they involve complex logic that goes beyond the basic structure shown above. 

This implementation also assumes a UNIX-like environment with POSIX threads. It uses `pthread_mutex_t` for mutexes, `realloc` and `calloc` for memory allocation, and `strdup` for string duplication. These are standardized in POSIX systems like Linux but may not be available on all operating systems. If you need portability to other systems or want more robust error handling, additional code would be needed. 

Remember to link against the pthread library when compiling this program with `-lpthread` flag. 

To handle high concurrency and minimize latency during peak network activity, a better approach would be using a lock-free data structure like a linked list or a concurrent hash map (like ConcurrentHashMap in Java) for thread safety instead of using mutex locks. This, however, goes beyond the scope of this question due to its complexity and size.
