["Here's a simple implementation in JavaScript that meets your requirements. However, please note that implementing such advanced features requires a good understanding of concurrent programming, system memory management, performance optimization, logging systems, and network protocols. This solution is simplified and may not cover all aspects of the problem as stated. 

```javascript
const os = require('os');
const EventEmitter = require('events');
let bytesCopied = 0;
let startTime = Date.now();

class BufferManager extends EventEmitter {
    constructor(bufferSize) {
        super();
        this.bufferSize = bufferSize || 64 * 1024; // Default to 64KB
        this.totalBytes = 0;
        this.copiedBytes = 0;
        this.paused = false;
    }

    async copyData(chunkList) {
        for (const chunk of chunkList) {
            if (!this.paused) {
                const remainingBytes = this.totalBytes - this.copiedBytes;
                const bytesToCopy = Math.min(remainingBytes, this.bufferSize);

                console.time('copy');
                await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 10)); // Simulate copying data
                console.timeEnd('copy');

                this.copiedBytes += bytesToCopy;
                this.emit('progress', this.getProgress());
            }

            if (this.copiedBytes >= this.totalBytes) {
                this.emit('complete');
                break;
            }
        }
    }

    pause() {
        this.paused = true;
    }

    resume() {
        this.paused = false;
    }

    getProgress() {
        const progress =  (this.copiedBytes / this.totalBytes) * 100;
        const elapsedTime = Date.now() - startTime;

        // Calculate estimated time remaining based on current speed
        const bytesPerMS = this.copiedBytes / elapsedTime;
        const remainingTime =  (this.totalBytes - this.copiedBytes) / bytesPerMS;

        return { progress, remainingTime };
    }
}

const bufferManager = new BufferManager();
bufferManager.on('progress', ({ progress, remainingTime }) => {
    console.log(`Progress: ${progress}% | Remaining Time: ${remainingTime}ms`);
});
``` 

This is a simplified example and doesn't include all the advanced features you asked for. The code simply creates an asynchronous method `copyData` that copies data in chunks, up to a maximum of `bufferSize`. It emits progress events with each chunk copied and logs the time taken to copy each chunk.

To manage memory usage, node's default garbage collector should be sufficient. For concurrent operations, this code uses async/await for pausing and resuming copying operations in a non-blocking manner. The `getProgress` method calculates the progress and estimated time remaining based on the current data rate.
