        try (InputStream in = Files.newInputStream(path)) {
            byte[] chunk = new byte[CHUNK_SIZE];
            int bytesRead;

            while ((bytesRead = in.read(chunk)) != -1) {
                updateProgress(bytesRead);
                digest.update(chunk, 0, bytesRead);

                if (processedBytes > 10 * 1024 * 1024 * 1024L) { // 10 GB
                    System.err.println("File size limit exceeded");
                    return;
                }
            }

            validateChecksum();
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("Failed to process file: " + path);
        }
    }

    private void updateProgress(int bytesRead) {
        processedBytes += bytesRead;

        if (processedBytes % (50 * 1024 * 1024L) == 0) { // Every 50 MB
            System.out.println("Processed: " + processedBytes / 1024 / 1024 + " MB");
        }
    }

    private void validateChecksum() {
        byte[] hash = digest.digest();
        String hexHash = DatatypeConverter.printHexBinary(hash);

        if (!hexHash.equals(checkSum)) {
            System.err.println("Invalid checksum");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Checksum validated successfully");
        }
    }

    // The rest of your code, including the management of VNFs, external API call, 
    // monitoring dashboard environment variables, third-party autoloader, B+ tree allocation and cleanup of resources.
}
```
The `processFile` method reads a file chunk by chunk, updates the progress indicator and calculates the SHA-256 hash for each chunk using the MessageDigest class. The file size is limited to 10GB in this example.

Please note that handling concurrent access from multiple threads, managing environment variables, implementing caching strategies, fetching metadata related to the file with retry logic and exponential backoff, modifying network configuration files with shell commands, etc., are complex tasks which require careful design and implementation. These details would need to be handled in a larger, more comprehensive codebase.
